# Loaded by `npm start` only. See .env.example for what each name means and
# for how to override these on a single machine (.env.local).

# Host-relative, same as the production build -- which only works because of
# src/setupProxy.js.
#
# The dev server is on :3000 and Apache is on :80, so a relative path would
# normally hit the dev server, which has no PHP. setupProxy.js forwards the two
# prefixes the app actually needs from Apache -- backend/api and backend/uploads
# -- and nothing else.
#
# It used to be the "proxy" field in package.json, which forwarded anything the
# dev server did not recognise. That is a reverse proxy into the entire XAMPP
# install, and because the forwarded request reached Apache from this machine it
# satisfied every `Require local` in the config: a tunnel on port 3000 therefore
# published phpMyAdmin, /server-status, /server-info, /xampp/ and /icons/ to the
# open internet. See src/setupProxy.js for the full account.
#
# This value is also what setupProxy.js derives the forwarded prefixes from, so
# changing the path here moves the allowlist with it.
#
# The point of doing it this way rather than naming an absolute URL is that the
# page and the API end up on the SAME origin as far as the browser is
# concerned. So `npm start` now works from any device that can reach this
# machine -- a phone on the same Wi-Fi, or a forwarded port -- instead of only
# from the machine itself. It also means no CORS preflight in development, and
# setupProxy.js sets changeOrigin so Apache is addressed as localhost on the way
# through, keeping the localhost allowance in configure_cors() satisfied.
REACT_APP_API_BASE_URL=/Capstone2/frontend/backend/api

# The dev server's host check rejects any request whose Host header is not this
# machine's LAN address -- which is exactly the port-forwarded case this setup
# was meant to support, hence this line.
#
# Note that with the "proxy" field removed from package.json the check is now
# off regardless of this line: react-scripts computes
# `disableFirewall = !proxy || DANGEROUSLY_DISABLE_HOST_CHECK`, and with no
# proxy field `!proxy` is already true. The line is kept because it states the
# intent, and because it keeps behaving the same if a proxy field ever returns.
#
# What the check defends against is DNS rebinding: a page on the open web
# pointing a hostname it controls at your address to read what the dev server
# serves. Losing it is a real, if narrow, loss.
#
# Which is worth restating after the 2026-08-24 penetration test: DO NOT PUBLISH
# THIS SERVER. A dev tunnel or forwarded port on :3000 is what put this project
# on the open internet. The dev server serves uncompiled source and a 13.8 MB
# source map, and it is not Apache, so not one .htaccess in this project applies
# to anything it answers -- every rule about directory listings, denied includes
# and security headers is simply absent. setupProxy.js closes the worst of it by
# no longer forwarding arbitrary paths to Apache, but it cannot make the dev
# server safe to expose, because that is not what a dev server is.
#
# To publish this application: `npm run build`, then serve frontend/build
# through Apache on port 80, where the .htaccess rules are real.
DANGEROUSLY_DISABLE_HOST_CHECK=true
